## Why This Is Asked

Introduces a practical runtime plugin scenario: logging backends and formatters can be swapped without redeploying, exercising registry and strategy patterns in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern
- Strategy/Adapter via pluggable backends and formatters
- Open/Closed principle and runtime configurability

## Code Example

```typescript
interface LogBackend {
  log(level: string, message: string, meta?: any): void;
}
interface Formatter {
  format(level: string, message: string, meta?: any): string;
}
class Logger {
  private backends = new Map<string, LogBackend>();
  private formatter: Formatter;
  constructor(formatter: Formatter) { this.formatter = formatter; }
  register(name: string, backend: LogBackend) { this.backends.set(name, backend); }
  unregister(name: string) { this.backends.delete(name); }
  setFormatter(f: Formatter) { this.formatter = f; }
  log(level: string, message: string, meta?: any) {
    const formatted = this.formatter.format(level, message, meta);
    for (const b of this.backends.values()) b.log(level, formatted, meta);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle backpressure when multiple backends write synchronously?
- How would you ensure log order and handle failures in a backend without losing logs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design extensible, runtime-configurable behavior with clean separation between policy and usage. It also probes how a candidate handles partial failures and ordering guarantees in distributed caches.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for runtime policies
- Registry/factory for dynamic policy loading
- Cache read/write interception and guarantees
- Failure-tolerance and backpressure considerations

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Policy<T> {
  onRead(key: string, value: T, context: any): T
  onWrite(key: string, value: T, context: any): T
}

class StrongPolicy<T> implements Policy<T> {
  onRead(k: string, v: T) { return v }
  onWrite(k: string, v: T) { return v }
}
class ReadYourWritesPolicy<T> implements Policy<T> {
  onRead(k: string, v: T) { /* ensure read reflects recent writes */ return v }
  onWrite(k: string, v: T) { return v }
}
class MonotonicPolicy<T> implements Policy<T> {
  onRead(k: string, v: T) { return v }
  onWrite(k: string, v: T) { return v }
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  static get<T>(name: string): Policy<T> {
    switch (name) {
      case 'Strong': return new StrongPolicy<T>()
      case 'ReadYourWrites': return new ReadYourWritesPolicy<T>()
      case 'Monotonic': return new MonotonicPolicy<T>()
      default: throw new Error('Unknown policy')
    }
  }
}

class CacheClient<T> {
  constructor(private policy: Policy<T>) {}
  get(key: string): T {
    const v = /* fetch from store */ (null as unknown as T)
    return this.policy.onRead(key, v, {})
  }
  put(key: string, value: T) {
    this.policy.onWrite(key, value, {})
    // store value
  }
}

const policy = PolicyRegistry.get<string>('ReadYourWrites')
const cache = new CacheClient<string>(policy)
```

## Follow-up Questions
